class Solution:
    def isSorted(self, nums):
        n = len(nums)
        if n <= 1:
            return True

        for start in range(n):
            sorted_rotation = True
            for step in range(1, n):
                current = nums[(start + step - 1) % n]
                following = nums[(start + step) % n]
                if current > following:
                    sorted_rotation = False
                    break

            if sorted_rotation:
                return True

        return False
```

Time complexity is `O(n^2)` in the worst case. Extra space is `O(1)`.

A sorted array that was rotated can have only one place where a value is bigger than the next value. The last value also connects back to the first, so the circular comparison is important.

For `[3, 4, 5, 1, 2]`, the only drop is `5 > 1`. The array is already sorted when there are zero drops. Equal values do not create a drop.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSorted(ArrayList<Integer> nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        if (n <= 1) {
            return true;
        }

        int drops = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int next = (i + 1) % n;
            if (nums.get(i) > nums.get(next)) {
                drops++;
                if (drops > 1) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

The input list is not changed. Time complexity is `O(n)` and extra space is `O(1)`.

Another way is to make a sorted copy first. Then every valid rotation of the input should match that copy when read from some starting index.

For example, the sorted copy of `[3, 4, 5, 1, 2]` is `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`. Starting at input index `3` produces exactly that order. The copy costs extra memory, but the comparison rule is straightforward.

