class Solution{
	public:
		bool isSorted(vector<int>& nums){
            int n = nums.size();
            if (n <= 1) {
                return true;
            }

            vector<int> sortedNums = nums;
            sort(sortedNums.begin(), sortedNums.end());

            for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
                bool matches = true;
                for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                    if (nums[(start + i) % n] != sortedNums[i]) {
                        matches = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (matches) {
                    return true;
                }
            }

            return false;
		}
};
```

A sorted copy is created, while the input vector stays unchanged. Sorting takes `O(n log n)`, and trying all starts takes `O(n^2)`, so the total time is `O(n^2)`. Extra space is `O(n)`.

This version joins the array to itself in a new vector. A rotation appears as a consecutive window of length `n` inside that doubled vector. I track the current non-decreasing run and return true when it reaches the needed length.

With `[3, 4, 5, 1, 2]`, the doubled values contain `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]` as one length-five window. An array such as `[2, 1, 3, 4]` never gets a sorted window of length four.

The input vector is not changed, but the doubled vector uses extra memory.

```cpp
class Solution{
	public:
		bool isSorted(vector<int>& nums){
            int n = nums.size();
            if (n <= 1) {
                return true;
            }

            vector<int> doubled;
            doubled.reserve(2 * n);
            for (int value : nums) {
                doubled.push_back(value);
            }
            for (int value : nums) {
                doubled.push_back(value);
            }

            int runLength = 1;
            for (int i = 1; i < 2 * n; i++) {
                if (doubled[i - 1] <= doubled[i]) {
                    runLength++;
                } else {
                    runLength = 1;
                }

                if (runLength >= n) {
                    return true;
                }
            }

            return false;
		}
};
```

Time complexity is `O(n)`, since the doubled vector is scanned once. Extra space is `O(n)` for that vector.

## Intuition
To check whether the array is sorted, compare each element with the element immediately before it. If we find any element that is smaller than the previous element, the array is not sorted. Otherwise, we continue checking the remaining elements recursively.

## Approach
Start checking from index 1 because the element at index 0 has no previous element.

For every index:

- Compare nums[i] with nums[i-1].
- If nums[i] < nums[i-1], return false.
- Otherwise, recursively check the next index.
- If we reach the end of the array, return true.

The vector is passed by reference so that no unnecessary copy of the array is created during each recursive call.

